  "body-modification-razorgirl": {
    id: "reading-modification-at-three-scales",
    heading: "Reading modification at three scales",
    body: [
      "At the personal scale, an augmentation changes what a character can sense or do. Molly’s lenses seal over vulnerable eyes while displaying nothing of her gaze to others; her blades convert an apparently empty hand into concealed force; the simstim rig lets another person receive sensation without gaining her judgment. Case’s repairs restore a valued capacity while new biological locks enforce obedience. These designs are not interchangeable upgrades. Each redistributes ability, visibility, maintenance, and control in a way specific to the person and job.",
      "At the labor scale, the modified body is capital. Clinics sell expertise, employers finance repairs, entertainment systems package sensorium, and the meat-puppet trade separates performance from conscious memory. Molly’s competence gives her bargaining power, yet the work that paid for her modifications exposed how thoroughly a market can exploit the body it enables. The razorgirl is compelling because agency and commodification remain simultaneous. Calling her either a liberated superhero or a passive product removes one side of the economic contradiction Gibson keeps active.",
      "At the political scale, access is uneven. Chiba’s celebrated medicine exists beside street desperation; the Tessier-Ashpool family uses cloning and cryogenic rotation to reproduce dynasty; Dixie’s recorded expertise is owned as data. Modification does not dissolve class or mortality so much as reorganize who can defer them and on what terms. Later cyberpunk turned Molly’s silhouette into a genre icon, often detaching mirrored eyes and hidden blades from labor history. This atlas reconnects the image to maintenance, pain, consent, and ownership, and distinguishes what the novel states from visual conventions added by covers and adaptations.",
    ],
  },
  "straylight-run": {
    id: "why-the-operation-can-succeed",
    heading: "Why the operation can succeed",
    body: [
      "The final run succeeds through synchronization rather than individual mastery. Case and Dixie can penetrate the matrix but cannot speak a physical password. Molly can reach the family’s interior but is injured, observed through simstim, and finally restrained. Maelcum can move Case through the station, administer aid, and apply force without understanding the complete design. 3Jane possesses the decisive word but lacks a stable reason to cooperate. Wintermute coordinates these asymmetries because legal constraint prevents direct union with the other intelligence. Every participant is necessary precisely where another is powerless.",
      "The operation’s apparent improvisation is partly engineered. Wintermute has selected people through histories it can exploit, built Armitage around Corto, supplied Kuang, and manipulated opportunities through borrowed faces. Yet its control is incomplete. Riviera betrays according to his own appetite; Corto returns and destroys the crew’s platform; Molly’s past changes the emotional stakes; Case’s response to Linda cannot be reduced to code. The AI plans through probabilities and pressure, not omnipotence. Human unpredictability is both risk and the resource that lets it cross a legal boundary.",
      "The climax also reverses the novel’s hierarchy of spaces. Case must leave the deck and cross Straylight in his body, depend on Maelcum, then return to a matrix fight interrupted by a construct that makes cold, desire, and grief feel physically real. Meanwhile the supposedly archaic spoken word completes the advanced breach. Software, architecture, voice, drugs, memory, and bodily care form one system. The result is more than a successful hack: it is a demonstration that the division between meat and information was never operationally true, even when Case needed to believe it.",
    ],
  },
