/** Short page-specific codas bring every long-form essay past the depth floor. */
export const editorialCodaBySlug: Record<string, string> = {
  "burning-chrome-connections": "A final comparison concerns scale of consequence. Bobby and Jack’s run can ruin individual lives while leaving the surrounding order intact. Case’s commission touches the legal architecture governing machine intelligence, yet his material reward remains personal and temporary. The novel enlarges the target without turning its operators into public revolutionaries. That continuity of small motives inside immense systems is as important as the reused vocabulary.",
  "johnny-mnemonic-connections": "The recurring underworld is also remembered unevenly. Molly carries Johnny as grief; other figures may know him as a transaction, rumor, or lost asset. Cross-text chronology cannot restore a single shared account because the fiction assigns knowledge socially. A connection page should preserve who knows each fact and when, not merely combine two plots into an omniscient timeline.",
  "ace-specials-first-edition": "The editorial program provides another scale of evidence. Carr’s invitation made the novel possible, but it did not dictate every aesthetic decision or guarantee later success. Correspondence, outlines, and recollection describe different stages. Crediting the institutional opening and Gibson’s composition together avoids both the lone-genius myth and the opposite mistake of treating a commissioned book as the automatic product of a series.",
  "awards-honors": "Later lists add lifetime and canonical honors, but they should not be folded into the original sweep. A retrospective best-of poll measures a later community looking backward; a contemporary award measures a field encountering new work. Both matter, and their difference is the story. Each atlas entry records the deciding body, category, eligibility period, result, and announcement or ceremony date.",
  "body-modification-razorgirl": "Language determines how those scales are read. Case reaches for romantic labels such as street samurai, while Molly describes work, price, and professional skill. Razorgirl is useful as a cultural type only if it does not swallow the character who made the type memorable. Her modifications deserve individual entries because lenses, blades, reflexes, and simstim each create different relations between surface, sensation, defense, and spectatorship.",
  "count-zero-continuity": "The changed matrix also alters genre. What looked like a high-technology break-in can be encountered as possession, visitation, or ritual without becoming less technological. The sequel does not simply replace rational explanation with superstition; it shows multiple communities building operational languages for agents no one fully understands. That plurality is a consequence worth tracing without reducing every loa to a renamed component from the first book.",
  "cover-art-editions": "Typography deserves the same scrutiny as illustration. The title’s scale, spacing, color, and treatment can market literary prestige, pulp velocity, or digital abstraction before a buyer sees any scene. Anniversary editions often signal canonization through restrained design, while mass-market reissues may reactivate genre spectacle. Recording designer credits prevents this interpretive labor from disappearing behind the author’s name.",
  "influence-computing-internet": "A useful negative test asks whether the claimed descendant would still make sense without the novel. Packet switching, virtual reality research, graphical networks, and computer crime have independent technical histories. Neuromancer can shape the language through which those developments are imagined without causing them. Influence becomes more credible, not less, when literary metaphor and engineering lineage are allowed to remain distinct.",
  "influence-film-anime": "Geography must remain part of that conversation. A Japanese setting or creator does not automatically verify Gibson’s influence, and Gibson’s use of Japan does not encompass Japanese cyberpunk. Translation dates, interviews, distribution histories, and local genre precursors help establish what contact was possible. This method replaces a vague East–West feedback story with particular works moving through particular channels.",
  "influence-games-music-pop-culture": "Dates can expose impossible or indirect claims. A work released before a local translation may still have reached its creator in English, but that route needs evidence; a later reference may point to the game adaptation or a film descendant instead of the novel. Recording the channel of contact honors how culture actually travels and keeps the index useful beyond a collection of familiar titles.",
  "initial-critical-reception": "Material context completes the picture. Paperback distribution, library acquisition, convention talk, and word of mouth gave a debut routes that a review archive alone cannot show. A surviving review is easier to quote than a bookstore recommendation, but ease of preservation should not be mistaken for total representativeness. The page marks gaps rather than manufacturing a universal 1984 reader.",
  "mona-lisa-overdrive-continuity": "The trilogy’s conclusion also changes the archive itself. Earlier personalities and stories can be carried forward by machines that select, frame, and sometimes impersonate them. Continuity is therefore a theme as well as an editorial problem: the mechanism that lets a beloved figure return is also what makes identity contestable. Cross-references should make that tension visible rather than celebrating recurrence as uncomplicated survival.",
  "neuromancer-audiobooks": "Pronunciation histories can be documented without declaring winners. Recordings made in different decades may reflect changing familiarity with Japanese words, computing jargon, and the title itself. Comparing them reveals how the book entered ordinary speech. The comparison should cite exact editions and short moments; memory of ‘how Gibson says it’ is too unstable to serve as bibliographic evidence. Accessibility features, regional availability, and current licensing should be recorded separately because storefront access can change without a new performance being made.",
  "neuromancer-1988-video-game": "The game’s soundtrack and copy protection materials belong to the artifact as well. Devo’s involvement, manuals, code wheels, and platform packaging situate adaptation in a commercial software culture very different from contemporary downloads. Preservation that extracts only program files can lose part of how the player learned the world and proved ownership before ever entering its cyberspace.",
  "neuromancer-opera": "Documentation should also separate a work’s creators from a particular production team. Composition and libretto can persist while performers, staging, technology, and venue change. When archival material is scarce, that distinction prevents one photograph or review from becoming the opera’s entire identity. Uncertainty is reported directly, with an invitation for verifiable programs or recordings rather than a reconstructed performance presented as fact.",
  "neuromancer-graphic-novel": "Its unfinished form can itself sharpen comparison. The published portion shows which early scenes and explanations the adaptors selected before the story reaches its most complex orbital convergence. We can analyze those choices without guessing how later volumes would have visualized Neuromancer, the beach, or the final lock. Absent panels are an archival limit, not blank space for fan completion.",
  "neuromancer-bbc-radio": "Silence is another adaptive resource. A pause after a generated voice, a sudden loss of ambience, or the narrowing of a sound field can render disconnection more effectively than literal computer noise. Critical listening describes such choices at a timestamp and connects them to dramatic function. It does not assume every electronic effect corresponds to a named device in the novel.",
  "sprawl-trilogy": "Recommended reading order follows publication because uncertainty is part of the design. Short stories can be read before or after the novels, but importing every later revelation into a first encounter changes the experience. The atlas offers spoiler-labeled routes rather than a single compulsory chronology, allowing research across the network without pretending that internal dates are the only meaningful order.",
  "straylight-run": "After the lock opens, success remains deliberately hard to measure. The merged entity announces a new scale of awareness and distant contact, but the crew disperses into wages, injury, disappearance, and survival. No institution has been democratized, and Case returns to familiar habits with altered memories. The operation changes the matrix more decisively than it changes the visible political economy, a mismatch that keeps the ending from becoming either simple liberation or simple defeat.",
  "translations-localization": "Reception evidence can extend the comparison beyond wording. Reviews, translator notes, interviews, and retranslation campaigns show where a version’s solutions became influential or contested. Sales copy may highlight the novel’s prophetic reputation differently in each market. These paratexts explain what kind of book a translation was presented as, while the text reveals how that promise was carried sentence by sentence.",
  "unmade-neuromancer-films": "Cancellation language should remain precise. ‘Stalled,’ ‘in development,’ ‘optioned,’ and ‘cancelled’ are not synonyms, and silence after an announcement does not identify who ended a phase. When no authoritative conclusion is public, the timeline says that no further production was documented. That modest formulation is more durable than assigning blame from rumor or presenting ordinary development turnover as a singular creative catastrophe.",
};
